  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">U.S.-MEXICO GRASSLAND BIRD CONSERVATION</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project continues work since 2002 to conserve high-priority and declining grassland bird species of western North America, in particular, Sprague&apos;s Pipit, Baird&apos;s Sparrow and Chestnut-collared Longspur, across their full annual cycle. Since 2012, in coordination with local partners, Bird Conservancy of the Rockies (BCR) has provided mid-term protection (15-year) and improved critical wintering habitat for grassland birds in the Chihuahuan Desert of northern Mexico through the Sustainable Grazing Network (SGN), a program designed to engage landowners in voluntary bird habitat conservation and management. To date, 28 ranches encompassing over 500,000 acres in four Grassland Priority Conservation Areas (GPCAs) are participating, and over 215,000 acres have been enhanced or restored. Partners monitor grassland birds and vegetation on SGN lands to quantify populations, measure outcomes and scale up results. In 2020, the SGN supported over 600,000 wintering grassland birds, despite widespread drought. On the other end of the annual cycle, BCR works with partners in Colorado to protect and manage important grasslands along the rapidly growing Front Range urban corridor that supports breeding and stopover for 20 high-priority grassland birds, including Chestnut-collared Longspur, Baird&apos;s Sparrow and 18 others that winter or stopover on SGN ranches in Chihuahuan Desert. In this next phase partners will: 1) expand the SGN to protect at least another 10,000 acres, 2) enhance at least 10,000 acres of grasslands across the SGN through improved grazing management, 3) restore at least 100 acres of degraded grasslands through shrub control or keyline plowing, 4) monitor grassland bird populations across the SGN, 6) conduct outreach to SGN partners to implement adaptive management, 7) permanently protect a key inholding (545.5 acres) of grasslands and shrublands in a large conservation area in Larimer County, Colorado, and 8) assist local governments in conserving grassland bir</narrative>
  </description>
